byte, which can represent many types of information, such as a letter of the alphabet, a decimal digit, or other character.</descrip></descripGrp><ntig><termGrp><term id="21562">binary digit</term><termNote type="partOfSpeech">Noun</termNote><termNote type="grammaticalNumber">Singular</termNote></termGrp></ntig></langSet><langSet xml:lang="as-IN"><ntig><termGrp><term id="432184">বিট</term><termNote type="geographicalUsage">IND</termNote><termNote type="partOfSpeech">Noun</termNote><termNote type="grammaticalGender">Neuter</termNote><termNote type="grammaticalNumber">Singular</termNote></termGrp></ntig></langSet></termEntry><termEntry id="4055_21562_432185"><langSet xml:lang="en-US"><descripGrp><descrip type="definition">The smallest unit of information handled by a computer or device. One bit expresses a 1 or a 0 in a binary numeral, or a true or false logical condition. A group of 8 bits makes up a byte, which can represent many types of information, such as a letter of the alphabet, a decimal digit, or other character.</descrip></descripGrp><ntig><termGrp><term id="21562">binary digit</term><termNote type="partOfSpeech">Noun</termNote><termNote type="grammaticalNumber">Singular</termNote></termGrp></ntig></langSet><langSet xml:lang="as-IN"><ntig><termGrp><term id="432185">বাইনেৰি ডিজিট</term><termNote type="geographicalUsage">IND</termNote><termNote type="partOfSpeech">Noun</termNote><termNote type="grammaticalGender">Neuter</termNote><termNote type="grammaticalNumber">Singular</termNote></termGrp></ntig></langSet></termEntry><termEntry id="4059_124417_1852602"><langSet xml:lang="en-US"><descripGrp><descrip type="definition">A file (or component within another file) that contains Automation standard descriptions of exposed objects, properties, and methods.</descrip></descripGrp><ntig><termGrp><term id="124417">type library</term><termNote type="partOfSpeech">Noun</termNote><termNote type="grammaticalNumber">Singular</termNote></termGrp></ntig></langSet><langSet xm